```
Registration: client creates a key pair (ideally in a hardware authenticator),
  sends attestation (optionally none), server stores:
    credentialId, publicKey (COSE), signCount, aaguid, transports, backupState,
    userVerified, attestationType, authenticatorData policy

Assertion:    server sends a WebAuthn challenge (>=16 random bytes, single use, ~5 min TTL)
  authenticator returns { authenticatorData, signature, userHandle }
  server verifies:
    1. challenge matches the stored, unexpired, unused challenge  -> else reject
    2. rpIdHash == SHA256(origin registrable domain suffix)         -> else reject
    3. flags UP (user present) set; UV required for privileged actions
    4. signature verifies under the stored public key over
       authenticatorData || SHA256(clientDataJSON)
    5. clientData.type == "webauthn.get" and .challenge == stored challenge
    6. clientData.origin == the exact expected origin
    7. signCount increased (detects cloned authenticators) when the authenticator is multi-device-capable
